\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 If this all triggers expansive, even mythic reveries in you, well, it does in me, too. Americans have been so raised on stories of self-reliance and self-ownership, the principles of personal responsibility and personal agency and personal autonomy, that it is hard to make room for any ecological influence without drawing on analogies that seem squishy, conspiratorial, New Agey, na\'efve.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 But I think of the immanent, capricious spirits of our polytheistic past or of the shadow stitched back onto the stockinged leg of Peter Pan. I think of the way that environmental conditions have so shaped the lives of humans that we know them now, from elementary school, as a species-scale evolutionary force \u8212  even while we tell ourselves, seemingly every day, that we are the masters of our own destinies.}
